  "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $10\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n10\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(10\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{21}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\times \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2}\\times \\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =4\\times 7 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 10\\,\\frac{1}{2}=28.\n$$",
